1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,440
Exam questions on DNA
and meiosis rarely ask

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:05,040
you to recite the steps.

3
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:07,840
They hand you something
new, a sequence written

4
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:10,640
from its 3' end, a copying error, a

5
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:12,960
cell you've never seen, and ask you to

6
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:13,520
trace it.

7
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:17,120
Every trace comes down
to three checks: which

8
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:20,400
strand, which end,
and what you're counting.

9
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:23,520
Here are four HSC-style questions.

10
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:26,080
Pause and try each one, then we'll work

11
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:28,000
it through one step at a time, and

12
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:29,600
look at the classic mistake.

13
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:31,680
Question one.

14
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:34,960
A bacterium is copying its DNA.

15
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:37,760
At one spot, the DNA has a T

16
00:00:37,840 --> 00:00:39,240
paired with an A.

17
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:42,880
Opposite that T, DNA
polymerase makes a rare

18
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:45,600
mistake: it adds a G instead of an

19
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:48,320
A, and the mistake is not repaired.

20
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:51,760
Both new molecules
are then copied once more,

21
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:53,200
with no more mistakes.

22
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:56,400
Of the four molecules, how many have a

23
00:00:56,480 --> 00:00:59,040
changed base pair at this spot, and what

24
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:00,240
is the changed pair?

25
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:02,080
Pause here to answer.

26
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:14,220
Start with the first copy.

27
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:16,980
The two strands separate, and each one is

28
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:17,980
a template.

29
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:20,580
The A strand gets a new T, so

30
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:22,340
that molecule is normal.

31
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:25,060
The T strand gets the wrong partner, a

32
00:01:25,140 --> 00:01:25,540
G.

33
00:01:26,180 --> 00:01:28,660
A T and a G aren't partners, so

34
00:01:28,740 --> 00:01:31,220
they pair badly, and this molecule has a

35
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:31,940
mismatch.

36
00:01:32,420 --> 00:01:34,260
It isn't a changed pair yet.

37
00:01:35,467 --> 00:01:36,586
Now copy again.

38
00:01:37,067 --> 00:01:39,946
The normal molecule makes
two normal molecules.

39
00:01:40,347 --> 00:01:42,346
The mismatched one separates too.

40
00:01:42,827 --> 00:01:44,986
Its T strand pairs with an A, as

41
00:01:45,067 --> 00:01:47,226
it should, so that copy is normal.

42
00:01:47,707 --> 00:01:49,946
But its G strand pairs with a C.

43
00:01:50,427 --> 00:01:52,346
So only one of the four molecules has

44
00:01:52,427 --> 00:01:55,466
a changed pair: G with C, where T

45
00:01:55,547 --> 00:01:56,746
with A used to be.

46
00:01:57,307 --> 00:01:59,466
The error took two rounds of copying to

47
00:01:59,547 --> 00:02:00,906
become a real change.

48
00:02:02,134 --> 00:02:04,933
The classic mistake is saying two, as if

49
00:02:04,974 --> 00:02:07,573
the mismatch copies itself
into both daughters.

50
00:02:07,974 --> 00:02:08,773
It doesn't.

51
00:02:09,334 --> 00:02:11,653
Each strand is a template, and the T

52
00:02:11,734 --> 00:02:14,373
strand still holds the right base, so its

53
00:02:14,454 --> 00:02:15,813
new copy is normal.

54
00:02:17,034 --> 00:02:17,833
Question two.

55
00:02:17,914 --> 00:02:21,593
Fireflies glow using
an enzyme made from one

56
00:02:21,674 --> 00:02:22,393
gene.

57
00:02:22,714 --> 00:02:24,873
Here's a short section
of that gene's coding

58
00:02:24,954 --> 00:02:27,513
strand, written from its 3' end to its

59
00:02:27,594 --> 00:02:28,833
5' end.

60
00:02:29,194 --> 00:02:32,793
Using the codon chart, write the mRNA with

61
00:02:32,914 --> 00:02:34,073
its ends.

62
00:02:34,234 --> 00:02:36,473
Then give the chain of amino acids this

63
00:02:36,674 --> 00:02:38,873
section codes for, and say how many there

64
00:02:38,954 --> 00:02:39,753
are.

65
00:02:39,914 --> 00:02:41,113
Pause here to answer.

66
00:02:51,534 --> 00:02:52,573
Two checks first.

67
00:02:52,974 --> 00:02:53,773
Which strand?

68
00:02:54,174 --> 00:02:57,533
It's the coding strand, so the mRNA is

69
00:02:57,614 --> 00:02:59,453
a copy of it, with U in place

70
00:02:59,534 --> 00:03:00,293
of T.

71
00:03:00,574 --> 00:03:01,613
No pairing.

72
00:03:01,694 --> 00:03:02,493
Which end?

73
00:03:02,734 --> 00:03:05,293
The mRNA runs the same way as the

74
00:03:05,494 --> 00:03:08,333
coding strand, so start at the strand's 5'

75
00:03:08,414 --> 00:03:09,933
end, on the right.

76
00:03:10,054 --> 00:03:13,133
Read right to left, T as U: A

77
00:03:13,294 --> 00:03:17,213
U G, U G G, A A A,

78
00:03:17,374 --> 00:03:19,773
U A A, G G C.

79
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:22,160
Now the chart.

80
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:25,640
A U G is the start codon, methionine.

81
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:29,480
U G G is tryptophan, and A A

82
00:03:29,560 --> 00:03:30,920
A is lysine.

83
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:33,720
U A A is a stop codon.

84
00:03:33,880 --> 00:03:35,800
It adds no amino acid, and the chain

85
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:38,840
is released, so G G C is never

86
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:39,240
read.

87
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:43,960
The chain is methionine,
tryptophan, lysine: three amino

88
00:03:44,040 --> 00:03:44,600
acids.

89
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:48,520
The classic mistake
is reading from the left,

90
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:50,280
because that's how we read English.

91
00:03:50,760 --> 00:03:54,360
That gives arginine,
asparagine and three more, with

92
00:03:54,520 --> 00:03:55,960
no start codon at all.

93
00:03:56,520 --> 00:03:59,320
Always find the 5' end first.

94
00:04:00,534 --> 00:04:01,733
Question three.

95
00:04:02,054 --> 00:04:06,053
A koala's body cells
each have 16 chromosomes.

96
00:04:06,134 --> 00:04:08,693
Before its DNA is copied, a cell in

97
00:04:08,734 --> 00:04:12,293
a koala's testis holds
16 chromosomes and about

98
00:04:12,374 --> 00:04:15,573
7.0 picograms of DNA.

99
00:04:15,654 --> 00:04:18,373
It then makes sperm by meiosis.

100
00:04:18,454 --> 00:04:20,533
Give the number
of chromosomes and the mass

101
00:04:20,614 --> 00:04:23,653
of DNA in this cell in prophase one,

102
00:04:23,774 --> 00:04:26,773
in each cell after meiosis one, and in

103
00:04:26,854 --> 00:04:28,453
each sperm cell.

104
00:04:28,614 --> 00:04:29,813
Pause here to answer.

105
00:04:40,234 --> 00:04:41,673
Use two rules.

106
00:04:41,914 --> 00:04:44,793
Count the chromosomes by
their centromeres, and count

107
00:04:44,874 --> 00:04:46,713
the DNA by the chromatids.

108
00:04:47,354 --> 00:04:50,873
Before prophase one, every
chromosome is copied into

109
00:04:50,954 --> 00:04:54,553
two sister chromatids, still
joined at one centromere.

110
00:04:55,114 --> 00:04:58,073
So the cell still has 16 chromosomes, but

111
00:04:58,154 --> 00:05:01,113
twice the DNA: 14 picograms.

112
00:05:01,754 --> 00:05:05,193
Copying doubles the DNA,
not the chromosomes.

113
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:10,160
Meiosis one separates
the homologous pairs, so each

114
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:12,200
cell gets 8 chromosomes.

115
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:15,840
But each chromosome still
has both its chromatids,

116
00:05:15,960 --> 00:05:19,040
so each cell holds half of 14: 7.0

117
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:21,080
picograms.

118
00:05:21,520 --> 00:05:24,640
Meiosis two splits the sister chromatids.

119
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:28,240
Each sperm cell keeps 8
chromosomes, now single,

120
00:05:28,320 --> 00:05:32,080
and half the DNA again: 3.5 picograms.

121
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:37,220
The classic mistake is 3.5
picograms after meiosis

122
00:05:37,300 --> 00:05:37,860
one.

123
00:05:38,100 --> 00:05:39,900
That's the sperm cell's amount.

124
00:05:40,180 --> 00:05:43,380
After meiosis one, each
chromosome still has two

125
00:05:43,460 --> 00:05:47,460
chromatids, so each cell still holds 7.0.

126
00:05:48,667 --> 00:05:49,626
Question four.

127
00:05:50,107 --> 00:05:52,826
In sweet peas, the gene for flower colour

128
00:05:52,987 --> 00:05:54,826
and the gene for pollen shape are on

129
00:05:54,907 --> 00:05:56,106
the same chromosome.

130
00:05:56,747 --> 00:05:58,906
A plant has the purple and long alleles

131
00:05:58,987 --> 00:06:01,626
on one chromosome, and the red and round

132
00:06:01,747 --> 00:06:03,546
alleles on its partner.

133
00:06:03,667 --> 00:06:05,946
In one cell in an anther, a single

134
00:06:06,027 --> 00:06:08,826
crossover happens between the two genes.

135
00:06:08,907 --> 00:06:10,666
List the alleles in each of the four

136
00:06:10,827 --> 00:06:12,586
pollen cells it makes.

137
00:06:12,907 --> 00:06:15,466
Then, if most anther
cells have no crossover

138
00:06:15,547 --> 00:06:19,066
between these genes, explain
why new combinations are

139
00:06:19,187 --> 00:06:21,146
much less common than half.

140
00:06:21,227 --> 00:06:22,506
Pause here to answer.

141
00:06:32,934 --> 00:06:34,533
Start with the chromatids.

142
00:06:34,774 --> 00:06:38,053
After copying, the pair
has four: two purple

143
00:06:38,134 --> 00:06:40,133
long, and two red round.

144
00:06:40,614 --> 00:06:43,333
A crossover swaps matching
pieces between two of

145
00:06:43,454 --> 00:06:46,293
them, one from each
partner, and only those

146
00:06:46,454 --> 00:06:46,933
two.

147
00:06:47,254 --> 00:06:50,453
Now there's one purple
long, one purple round,

148
00:06:50,574 --> 00:06:52,933
one red long and one red round.

149
00:06:54,134 --> 00:06:56,933
Meiosis one sends the two
partners to different

150
00:06:57,014 --> 00:07:00,133
cells, and meiosis two splits each pair of

151
00:07:00,214 --> 00:07:01,573
sister chromatids.

152
00:07:01,894 --> 00:07:05,013
So each pollen cell gets
one chromatid: purple

153
00:07:05,094 --> 00:07:08,853
long, purple round,
red long and red round.

154
00:07:09,174 --> 00:07:12,133
Two are the plant's original
combinations, and two

155
00:07:12,214 --> 00:07:12,853
are new.

156
00:07:13,334 --> 00:07:15,973
So this one cell made
four different pollen

157
00:07:16,054 --> 00:07:16,613
cells.

158
00:07:17,834 --> 00:07:20,393
So why are new combinations much less than

159
00:07:20,474 --> 00:07:21,233
half?

160
00:07:21,514 --> 00:07:23,593
Even in this cell, only two of the

161
00:07:23,674 --> 00:07:24,753
four are new.

162
00:07:24,954 --> 00:07:27,433
And as the card says, most cells have

163
00:07:27,594 --> 00:07:30,553
no crossover between
these two genes, so all

164
00:07:30,634 --> 00:07:33,193
four keep the original combinations.

165
00:07:33,274 --> 00:07:35,993
The classic mistake
is saying all four pollen

166
00:07:36,074 --> 00:07:37,273
cells are new.

167
00:07:37,314 --> 00:07:40,073
The crossover only touched two chromatids.

168
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:43,780
So here's how to start any trace.

169
00:07:44,020 --> 00:07:47,300
A sequence: ask which
strand, then find the

170
00:07:47,460 --> 00:07:49,700
5' end before you read.

171
00:07:49,940 --> 00:07:52,900
A copying error: follow each strand as its

172
00:07:52,980 --> 00:07:53,980
own template.

173
00:07:54,180 --> 00:07:58,700
A count: centromeres for
chromosomes, chromatids for DNA.

174
00:07:58,900 --> 00:08:02,980
A crossover: it changes
two chromatids, not four.

175
00:08:03,060 --> 00:08:05,780
Next: how a chain of amino acids becomes

176
00:08:05,860 --> 00:08:06,580
a trait.

177
00:08:07,800 --> 00:08:09,240
Here are the key notes.

178
00:08:09,480 --> 00:08:11,400
Pause the video if you need more time

179
00:08:11,480 --> 00:08:12,280
to copy them.
